# librepl89

A small C89 interactive editor for one arbitrary multiline UTF-8 submission on
a modern POSIX terminal.

## Scope

- POSIX tty raw-mode handling, `TIOCGWINSZ`, bracketed paste.
- UTF-8 multiline input with LF as the logical line boundary and HT as
  content.
- Cursor editing by extended grapheme cluster (via `libu89`).
- Visual wrapping, continuation prompts, complete redisplay.
- In-memory history of whole submissions.
- Blocking (`repl89_read`) and event (`repl89_feed`) interfaces.
- Resize and temporary hide/show for interleaved application output.

Not provided: parsing, completeness detection, syntax highlighting,
completion, persistent history, configurable key maps, vi mode, mouse support,
terminfo/termcap, legacy terminals, or word segmentation.

## Boundary

```text
libu89       what Unicode text means
librepl89    how an editable Unicode submission behaves on a terminal
application  what the submission means to the language or REPL
```

## Build and test

    just build      # build libu89 and librepl89.a
    just test       # smoke + unit suite
    just check      # green gate: C89∩C23, GCC+Clang, tidy checks, format

## Examples

    just echo           # blocking echo REPL
    just echo-events    # event-driven echo REPL with SIGWINCH handling

`examples/echo.c` is a complete blocking client: it reads one submission at a
time with `repl89_read`, echoes it byte-for-byte, keeps whole submissions in
history, and exits on EOF (Ctrl-D on an empty line). A blocking client reacts
to a terminal resize on its next interaction.

`examples/echo_events.c` is the event-driven counterpart: it owns a trivial
`SIGWINCH` handler that sets a flag, observes the flag around `repl89_feed`,
and calls `repl89_resize`, so a resize redraws immediately even while feed is
blocked. The library never installs signal handlers itself.

## Dependencies

`librepl89` links the sibling `libu89` (`../libu89`), which must be built
first (`just build` does this).

## Conventions

- Strict ISO C89, four-space indent, functional style, Allman format.
- Green-clean: C89 AND C23 under GCC and Clang, green semantic checks.
- Test-driven: scenario -> acceptance -> failing test -> code -> refactor.
  Scenarios, stories, design, and acceptance live under `.agent/`.
